      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p4_r1"
      },
      "text": "The stage two payload that was analyzed in this report (dc9b5e8aa6ec86db8af0a7aa897ca61db3e5f3d2e0942e319074db1aaccfdc83), after launching the infected version of CCleaner, was dropped to only a selective group of targets, as reported by Talos. Although there is an x64 version, the following analysis will only include the x86 version because they are nearly identical. I will not be going too far in depth as full comprehension of the technical analysis will require an understanding of reverse engineering.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_25",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Intezer_Evidence-Aurora-Supply-Chain-Attack-CCleaner-Part2(10-02-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 6,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p6_r1"
      },
      "text": "Depending on if you’re running x86 or x64 Windows, it will drop a different module. (32-bit 07fb252d2e853a9b1b32f30ede411f2efbb9f01e4a7782db5eacf3f55cf34902, 64-bit 128aca58be325174f0220bd7ca6030e4e206b4378796e82da460055733bb6f4f) Both modules are actually legitimate software with additional code and a modified execution flow.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_27",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Intezer_Evidence-Aurora-Supply-Chain-Attack-CCleaner-Part2(10-02-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 12,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p12_r1"
      },
      "text": "The next payload is executed, which decrypts another module and loads it. If we look at the memory of the next decrypted payload, we can see something that looks like a PE header without the MZ signature. From here, it is as simple as modifying the first two bytes to represent MZ and we have a valid PE file. (f0d1f88c59a005312faad902528d60acbf9cd5a7b36093db8ca811f763e1292a)",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_28",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Intezer_Evidence-Aurora-Supply-Chain-Attack-CCleaner-Part2(10-02-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 16,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p16_r1"
      },
      "text": "The value is then XOR decrypted by 0x31415926 which gives you 0x5A093B0D or the IP address: 13.59.9.90",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
